  "answer": "Phototropism in plants occurs when the plant bends towards a light source. This response is due to the plant's ability to sense light and the distribution of a chemical called auxin. When light hits one side of the plant, more auxin accumulates on the shaded side. Auxin causes the cells on the shaded side to grow faster, making the plant bend towards the light. This was demonstrated by Charles Darwin and his son Francis Darwin, who found that the tip of the plant (coleoptile) is responsible for sensing light. Further experiments by F.W. Went showed that the bending is caused by a chemical stimulus, which is now known as auxin. The auxin is produced in the tip of the plant and moves down to the stem, causing the cells on the shaded side to elongate more, leading to the bending of the stem towards the light.",
